    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Whittington sprang up in the 1870s when the Chicago & Eastern Illinois Railroad cut through Franklin County, turning a quiet prairie into a bustling rail hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/whittington_illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was named for Colonel William Lee Davidson Ewing, a Black Hawk War veteran who later served as Illinois state auditor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/whittington_illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Whittington hosts the headquarters of Wayne Fitzgerrell State Recreation Area, a 3,300‑acre park that surrounds Rend Lake.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/whittington_illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Located at 38°05′21″N 88°54′10″W, Whittington sits 443 feet above sea level in southern Illinois.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/whittington_illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Whittington experiences a humid subtropical climate, with hot, humid summers and mild winters.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/whittington_illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Rend Lake, created in 1973, cuts flood peaks on the Big Muddy by up to 80%, protecting surrounding lowlands.",
